From 156156eda36dd6a3c7e02c43a5802f71d5dcb5b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davidson Francis <davidsondfgl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:17:27 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Flush TLB for newly EXEC'ed memory (#2414)

* Flush TLB for newly EXEC'ed memory

Commit 56d010b (PR #2337) added a conditional check so that
the 'TLB_NOTDIRTY' flag was set only for executable pages,
since it was used solely to identify the first write to
pages with an associated TB.

However, that commit introduced a bug: for pages where
execute permission was set at runtime and which already
had a TLB entry, the 'TLB_NOTDIRTY' flag was not updated;
consequently, Unicorn executed code using the old TB
instead of the updated one.

This commit fixes the issue by adding a TLB flush in
uc_mem_protect() whenever the 'UC_PROT_EXEC' flag
is added.

Fixes: 56d010b ("cputlb: set TLB_NOTDIRTY flag only for executable pages")
Reported-by: Viktor Babrian <viktor.babrian@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Davidson Francis <davidsondfgl@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Viktor Babrian <viktor.babrian@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: PhilippTakacs <philipp.takacs@iosb.fraunhofer.de>

* Re-arm TLB_NOTDIRTY for new TBs instead of flushing the TLB

Since 6a790682 ("cputlb: set TLB_NOTDIRTY flag only for executable
pages (#2337)"), a write TLB entry filled while its page was not yet
executable lacks TLB_NOTDIRTY.  tb_gen_code() re-arms it through
tlb_reset_dirty_by_vaddr(), but skipped that whenever a memory hook was
installed, because notdirty_write() keeps TLB_NOTDIRTY set in that case.
With a memory hook, writes to such a page thus never invalidated the
TBs translated from it (#2367).

Always re-arm TLB_NOTDIRTY in tb_gen_code() instead of flushing the
whole TLB in uc_mem_protect() when EXEC is added.  The flush broke
uc_mem_protect() calls from memory hooks: the load/store helpers keep
using the TLB entry they looked up before running the hooks, which the
flush resets to -1 or frees when it resizes the TLB, so the access went
to host address guest_addr - 1 or read freed memory.

Add tests that make a page executable from a UC_HOOK_MEM_FETCH_PROT
and a UC_HOOK_MEM_WRITE hook, check uc_mem_protect() in test_smc2 and
drop its unused register initializations.

Fixes: 6a790682 ("cputlb: set TLB_NOTDIRTY flag only for executable pages (#2337)")

* Re-arm TLB_NOTDIRTY in uc_mem_protect instead of in tb_gen_code

The previous commit made tb_gen_code() always call
tlb_reset_dirty_by_vaddr(), which scans the whole TLB.  That scan used
to be skipped while a memory hook covers the code, so it noticeably
slowed down translation for such users (2.4x on aarch64 when translating
many small blocks with a global UC_HOOK_MEM_UNMAPPED hook).

Restore tb_gen_code() and instead set TLB_NOTDIRTY again in
uc_mem_protect() when a RAM range gains UC_PROT_EXEC, using QEMU's
existing tlb_reset_dirty().  It matches the TLB entries by host address,
so it also covers any virtual address the range is mapped at, and it
only sets a flag on them.  Unlike the TLB flush of the original fix,
that keeps the entries valid, so it is safe to call from memory hooks,
and the translation and execution paths are not touched.

---------

